WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.240
Little League Baseball Championship Series!

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:07.260
It's the game every kid in America loves to play!

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:30.640
NOSTALGIA And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.940
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:40.880
And I'm Joe. Now, is Little League Baseball, like the idea of that, is that the game that kids in America love to play?

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:44.660
Or is it this particular video game?

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.080
Because, you know, it's saying, it's very trickily worded there,

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:53.780
it's the game every kid in America loves to play, now, as a video game?

00:00:54.300 --> 00:01:00.420
I was going to call them out on that, but it sounds like now that you're saying this, they've covered their asses.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:05.960
Yes. Because I was like, you can't know that every kid in America loves to play your game before you release your game.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:10.360
But yeah, they're saying like, oh no, no, we meant Little League Baseball, just in general.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.000
So, clever on you.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.420
It's definitely ambiguous enough to be taken as both.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:23.920
Right. A classic marketing doublespeak, which transitions right into a pledge.

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:38.100
A pledge. This is the first time, sorry, I just, I wanted to call it out right away, but this is the first time that a, like a patriotic pledge has been put in front of me when I boot up a game.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.100
I thought it was very charming.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:49.120
Well, because this is different than a game we played pretty recently.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:52.480
I know Sean didn't really want to see a baseball game too soon, but.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:59.080
No, I didn't. We did play a softball game pretty recently that had a recreational component to it.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:04.400
And I don't know, my days of playing Little League Baseball were pretty recreational.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:18.980
Um, but this is more like Little League World Series to the point where even the, the teams in the game, there's 16 of them, are like cities in America and like major countries around the world.

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:20.500
Like it's pretty serious.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:26.640
And I don't know if Italy even has a Little League Baseball thing, but they do in this game.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.640
That's what I was going to ask.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:37.580
Like I've seen, I've seen like a Little League World Series on TV, but it's all almost always just like the final series.

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:48.260
And it's usually just like US first Puerto Rico or some Latin American country are does each like state get a team too?

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:56.620
Oh yeah. So that, that's how it works is like first the Little League World Series, like does the, does like the preliminary roundup.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:03:00.260
Where the local, like let's say New York State, right?

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:07.580
All of the local New York teams battle it out to be like, who's going to be the representative from New York State?

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:12.880
And then they get to become that one team that represents New York.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:18.760
Oh wow. And then like the 50 team face off that then becomes like, well, who's going to be the one to be Team USA?

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:22.140
So it is pretty freaking competitive when you actually think about it.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:23.980
That is a lot of, that's a lot of baseball.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:36.560
But I was, what I didn't, you know, realize, or I don't know if this is accurate or not, but the, you know, you've got New York, you've got LA, you've got Tokyo, then you've got Canada, just Canada.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:42.840
Well, it's also, it's just, it's not any city, it's all states or countries.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:45.100
Yeah. Oh yeah, yeah, that's right.

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:47.720
So it's not. It's not Los Angeles and it's not Tokyo.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:49.020
Right, that's right. It's California and Japan.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:52.920
It's California. Yeah. But then Canada, all of Canada, that's just, it's not provinces.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:56.680
I think it's just like, you know, it's America's pastime.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:58.720
So we get all the, we get all the teams.

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:12.340
Yeah, I really don't know how it works in the other countries, Joe, but I would be surprised to find out that Canada, you know, would have the same style thing where like, okay, all the provinces got to like duke it out first.

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:14.700
Yeah. And do they have to sell the pledge?

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:23.180
I'm saying there's a lot less, there's a lot, there's probably a lot fewer people like competing to get into it in Canada because they're a hockey country and we're a baseball country.

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:25.700
But we can also be a hockey country.

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:29.400
In fact, when was the last time those losers even had the Stanley Cup?

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:33.460
Yeah, but I didn't say we're, I didn't say we're not a hockey country.

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:34.880
I said they're a hockey country.

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:39.860
See, but just not to go on a tangent, but I, since I got the idea in my head, I have to say it out loud now.

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:53.860
That is the actual way to steal art from other countries is beat them so bad that you can take their trophy from them and tell them that they can have it back if they, you know, if they're willing to beat you in their preferred sport.

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:56.240
But they continue to not do that.

00:04:56.460 --> 00:05:04.480
And meanwhile, the Mona Lisa sits comfortably in Paris, even though they did nothing sportsmanlike to get that.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:10.500
I appreciate your uncalled for trash talk.

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:13.540
But, but that's why I appreciate it.

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:21.020
Great. So, okay, back to Little League Baseball Championship Series, which I don't think I have to say that full title every time.

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:23.760
I think we can just say Little League from this point forward.

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:26.260
Before we can talk about the game.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:30.040
Before we can talk about the game, we all, we all participated in Little League.

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:31.060
Am I right or am I wrong?

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:32.740
No. You are correct.

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:34.840
Is it for my part of that?

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:39.260
Yeah. Sean said no, but I didn't ask a, I didn't ask a yes or no question actually.

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:41.280
No, you're correct. Sean played.

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:43.620
I did not play. He was on my team.

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:47.220
Sean did not play, but he was on Joe's baseball team.

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:52.660
Sean, do you feel like you wanted to play and that your parents didn't let you play?

00:05:52.660 --> 00:06:00.820
No, I had no interest until like high school to play any kind of, any kind of team sport.

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:10.840
This is why every city in America needs a major league baseball team because tragedies like this can happen to even good friends of ours because they just don't have.

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:12.880
I mean, Joe, I'm, I'm curious.

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:17.640
How did you get into Little League baseball if there wasn't a major league baseball team around?

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:21.320
So when you're saying Little League baseball, you said I'm like the organization of Little League.

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:23.240
Like I played. No, I'm talking about playing.

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:32.480
Yeah, I guess I guess I am talking a little bit about like an organization of sorts, but like I wasn't part of an organization, but not, not like, not like the low level of this Little League.

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:33.580
I mean, I just played. Oh yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:34.700
I never traveled.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:36.660
Yeah. But what's your question?

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:38.560
Like, how did I, like, where do you think I live?

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:40.720
We don't have baseball?

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:41.980
Like Buffalo.

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:45.580
Well, allegedly Sean never saw baseball until he moved to New York City.

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:47.200
I never said that.

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:48.400
I said that for you.

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:49.460
You're making that up.

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:51.480
We've got a triple A farm team here.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:55.000
Yeah. That's true. I went to baseball games.

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:58.540
I just didn't care about sports until later in life.

00:06:58.700 --> 00:06:59.980
Okay. Then that's a good idea.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:01.360
You know, Mike, I, I'll tell you this too.

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:02.340
I ate pizza too.

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:07.700
All right. That's surprising, but you didn't eat good pizza until you moved to New York, which is

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:09.220
kind of true about it. New York has shit pizza.

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:11.680
Buffalo has different pizza than New York City, but it's good pizza.

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:13.760
Wait, Sean, what'd you say?

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:15.880
What did you say?

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:21.200
Okay. Um, so Sean, going back to your whole, like I wasn't into sports when I was a kid.

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:23.180
Did you own sports games?

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:25.860
Oh yeah, but never baseball.

00:07:26.140 --> 00:07:29.260
Got it. Okay. So this is just, you have an extreme bias towards baseball.

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:31.540
So I don't even know if you're eligible for the essential games list at this point.

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:34.300
Well, yeah, I voted on the other baseball games.

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:35.340
That was a softball game.

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:37.580
So. Oh, oh yeah. The other ones before that.

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:38.520
Well, I didn't know that. The other ones.

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:39.500
The dozens.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:41.820
I didn't have that information in front of me.

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:43.180
Now you're revealing a lot of stuff.

00:07:43.420 --> 00:07:47.240
Well, okay. If that's a, sorry, we're going to, we're going to make this personal now.

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:50.660
If that's the case, you guys can't vote on any other sport.

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:52.840
That's fine. Yeah.

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:54.240
I don't even want to show up for those.

00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:56.980
Yeah. All right.

00:07:57.060 --> 00:07:57.940
To talk about the game.

00:07:57.940 --> 00:08:03.680
There is, there is something unique about little league baseball that we haven't seen in the baseball games that we played so far.

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:10.300
And that is that the home team is determined by a drop of the bat.

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:12.720
And it's a little mini game.

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:13.740
That's made up, right?

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:14.940
That is totally made up.

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:16.880
I've never seen that. Okay, good.

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:19.560
What? I mean, I say totally made up, but.

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:23.140
Oh, you mean the drop, you've never seen like them drop the bat like that?

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:29.720
I've never seen an umpire hold a bat out and whoever catches the closest to the nub gets to be the home team.

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:37.020
I've never seen that in any like official capacity, but that's like, that's like, you know, that's like your pickup baseball game style.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:38.460
I feel like that's pretty common. Yeah.

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:41.000
But at that point, it's like, you know, why don't you just spin the bat too?

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:43.420
And whoever it points at, they're the home team, right?

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:45.620
Like it. Oh, all right.

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:46.720
Let, let me ask you this.

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:50.700
You're, you're, you're with your, with your friends in your backyard and you're like, oh, let's play baseball.

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:52.300
How do you pick the home team?

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:54.720
You draw sticks. That's a lot of sticks.

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:56.020
You gotta get. You already have a bat right there.

00:08:56.360 --> 00:08:57.840
How many teams are there?

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:01.320
Hold on a minute. How many, how many teams are there?

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:04.640
When you say draw sticks, you just put two sticks and you just, you just draw one.

00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:09.700
Well, yeah. Whoever, and whoever's got the, whoever pulls out the one bat.

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:12.280
I'm not talking about bats. I'm talking about sticks in the yard.

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:13.920
Oh, sticks in the yard.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:15.180
Whoever has the bigger sticks.

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:17.800
For some reason I was thinking you had like a handful of sticks and you keep going until

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:18.760
someone gets the short one.

00:09:18.820 --> 00:09:20.780
I don't know why. I was just, I was just elevating it.

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:24.040
Yeah. No, that's funny. But I also like that we could call bat sticks around here.

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:27.280
Yeah. That's for reserved for stickball.

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:30.000
I'm sure we'll get an NES stickball game at some point.

00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:32.960
I can't wait. Oh, which does have its own set of rules too, actually.

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:36.020
So there is some nuance between stickball, softball and baseball.

00:09:36.820 --> 00:09:39.560
This is what you get when we're on our like 40th baseball game.

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:41.340
Like what else can we talk about at this point?

00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:48.300
Well, you know what? There is actually something to talk about and it's the presentation of the same game over

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:53.740
and over again, because yes, mechanically, this is not doing anything different.

00:09:53.920 --> 00:10:00.820
Although I do feel like the swing results in a lot more check swings than other baseball

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:02.000
games we've played on the NES.

00:10:02.120 --> 00:10:03.000
I'm not sure what that's about.

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:04.780
Maybe I'm just not committing.

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:07.800
Yeah, because you have to hold the button a lot longer.

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:10.700
Yes. As soon as you let go of the button, he'll stop in the middle of the swing.

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:11.820
I'm not used to that.

00:10:11.960 --> 00:10:16.860
I'm not used to like the idea that the NES has like, you know, obviously it does because

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:20.620
we know from the jump in Super Mario Brothers, we know as early as that, that it like has

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:27.040
a responsiveness. But I'm used to just like you press the A to do the A button thing, not like, oh, you

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:29.160
follow through on your swing.

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:30.700
Right. You should. So that was cool.

00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:34.500
But I meant actually just the presentation because, you know, we've always applauded

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:38.760
the ones that get close to the TV broadcast.

00:10:39.020 --> 00:10:40.700
But this doesn't really go for that.

00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:46.100
But what it what it misses in that I think it really made up for in terms of user interface.

00:10:46.280 --> 00:10:47.520
You get a lot of information.

00:10:47.740 --> 00:10:51.000
Yeah. And and it's all like neatly displayed.

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:56.780
And the picture is pushed like as if he's further back away that like it's not it's not

00:10:56.780 --> 00:10:57.840
a busy screen.

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:03.260
I mean, you also have like all the base runners, which should probably go away if there are

00:11:03.260 --> 00:11:08.340
no base runners. It's a little information, but you have all the base runners up in the top right and you

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:13.360
can see them and they can't lead off the base, which would be the most interesting thing

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:14.760
to see in that camera.

00:11:14.780 --> 00:11:20.860
But it's nevertheless, it's nice to see them instead of some like small diamond with little

00:11:20.860 --> 00:11:25.140
like baseball helmets on the areas where the runners are.

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:26.780
It is nice.

00:11:27.140 --> 00:11:32.860
But yeah, with without them leading off, it's it feels kind of unnecessary to me.

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:33.720
You get to see them.

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:36.600
So, yeah, it's extraneous a bit.

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:42.240
But I think like what what I like about this, the game's presentation in general, and it's

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:43.740
not just like the UI.

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:50.820
It's the fact that this this game just feels a lot smoother to like between actually presenting

00:11:50.820 --> 00:12:00.040
all the information that it thinks you need and the the perspective not resulting in this

00:12:00.040 --> 00:12:08.340
super cartoonish, like a bunch of like tiny Mario's jumping around and actually kind of

00:12:08.340 --> 00:12:10.540
looks like people out in the field.

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:17.640
It makes it it gives it a much different vibe like this is kind of like a sim game, even

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:24.220
though it's not really. And I know we've talked about this in the past, but I totally agree with how smooth the

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:29.700
game is. And I actually felt like in this particular version of baseball, I was actually able to

00:12:30.620 --> 00:12:32.880
maybe it's because of how far back they put the pitcher.

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:37.960
I was able to tell if things were sometimes a ball before just swinging blindly at them.

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:40.720
So I appreciate this game on like a whole nother level for that.

00:12:41.800 --> 00:12:48.140
But it still has that same issue with the the pop up where sometimes, you know, I'm sure

00:12:48.140 --> 00:12:50.260
if I would have played over and over again, I would have got better at it.

00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:55.020
But sometimes you just don't track the ball because the ball has a shadow, but the players

00:12:55.020 --> 00:12:56.120
don't have shadows.

00:12:56.340 --> 00:12:58.680
Well, that's a huge issue in this game.

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:05.640
Well, you know, we I wanted to get it out of the way that I I really like the look and

00:13:05.640 --> 00:13:14.200
feel, but it's almost like they went for look and feel over utility because there's no way

00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:15.640
to track where your fielders are.

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:20.040
You just kind of have to you just have to hope they're there or like hope you're holding

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:28.640
down the right button because like there's even like a little like base runner like that

00:13:28.640 --> 00:13:30.660
where you're tracking your base runners.

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:36.080
There's almost like that mini map down there where you could just have a bunch of dots like,

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:37.740
oh, here's where your fielders are.

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:39.080
But that doesn't exist.

00:13:39.260 --> 00:13:44.720
You just kind of you can't see them because you're so zoomed in to get this more realistic

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:49.220
view that you just don't have the information that you need to field correctly.

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:55.340
Yeah, that was my biggest pet peeve by far as well, which it often is in these baseball

00:13:55.340 --> 00:14:01.100
games, especially because the computer doesn't like adjust for it either.

00:14:01.400 --> 00:14:03.900
So like when the computer is in the field, they're perfect at it because they know where

00:14:03.900 --> 00:14:10.200
they are. Yeah, but I almost could never catch a pop fly because I don't know where I don't know where

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:12.520
my like left fielder is until it's too late.

00:14:20.900 --> 00:14:27.260
We had a discussion on the softball episode about how many innings Little League baseball

00:14:27.260 --> 00:14:34.000
is. And I believe I wasn't speaking for this game, but I might have said seven.

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:39.700
In this game, there are six before a winner is determined, but it can go up to as many

00:14:39.700 --> 00:14:43.640
as 18 innings for extra innings if there's a tie.

00:14:43.880 --> 00:14:46.720
And I know for a fact that that's not how it works.

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:52.100
You know, I know that they don't do extra innings to that extent for Little League.

00:14:52.360 --> 00:15:00.420
But apparently in like in competitive Little League baseball World Series or whatever this

00:15:00.420 --> 00:15:02.540
is championship series, it's six.

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:05.160
And then like in regular play, it's seven.

00:15:05.340 --> 00:15:11.180
So I wonder why actually you would lose an inning for the more competitive one.

00:15:11.320 --> 00:15:13.520
But maybe, you know, that's an interesting twist, right?

00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:14.900
What if it was just one inning?

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:22.540
What if baseball was just each person gets three outs and they, you know, and if it's tied

00:15:22.540 --> 00:15:26.260
after two, if it's tied after the first inning, you go into the second inning, you know?

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:34.380
And so if you're pretty far back on like in the the lineup, you might not see the you might

00:15:34.380 --> 00:15:37.520
not see the field for like a few games.

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:38.620
Yeah, absolutely.

00:15:38.660 --> 00:15:43.240
Or, you know, this is like this is like a different version where instead of like, you know, there's

00:15:43.240 --> 00:15:44.440
five on five basketball.

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:48.400
But then there's also, you know, even in the Olympics now they have three on three basketball

00:15:49.060 --> 00:15:51.100
like street basketball in the Olympics.

00:15:51.740 --> 00:15:53.120
And why not for baseball?

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:57.300
It's like, you know, it's four V I would, I would watch, I would watch three V three,

00:15:57.540 --> 00:16:00.100
like a pitcher, a catcher and one fielder.

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:05.680
That'd be pretty cool. So I recently went to a Savannah bananas game.

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:08.200
This didn't happen at the game that I went to.

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:12.740
If anyone's not aware, Savannah bananas is kind of like the Harlem Globetrotters of baseball,

00:16:12.740 --> 00:16:14.600
but like they play a real game.

00:16:14.600 --> 00:16:21.620
But apparently their tiebreaker instead of extra innings is they have they have a pitcher,

00:16:22.560 --> 00:16:25.100
a batter, and then the pitcher has one fielder.

00:16:26.680 --> 00:16:32.720
And there's one at bat and you hit the ball, you get on base or whatever.

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:36.400
If you don't if you're not out or you don't score, then you switch.

00:16:36.620 --> 00:16:40.160
The other team now has a batter and the first team has a pitcher and a fielder.

00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:42.860
And you keep doing that until someone either scores or gets out.

00:16:42.980 --> 00:16:44.260
It's like a shootout for baseball.

00:16:45.120 --> 00:16:46.060
Well, yeah, it's insane.

00:16:46.960 --> 00:16:48.680
I love it. That sounds great.

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:50.320
And that's exactly my thought Joe was a shootout.

00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:55.980
But for baseball, I, yeah, all these things, guys, just MLB, give us a call, whatever you want.

00:16:56.160 --> 00:16:58.280
We, we know how to make the game more exciting for kids.

00:16:58.460 --> 00:17:02.640
