But as Italy this time, right?

00:20:28.080 --> 00:20:29.960
Because, like, they're the known weakest.

00:20:30.200 --> 00:20:33.340
Or, you know, uh, first time playing, who should I be?

00:20:33.340 --> 00:20:35.860
If I want to just be good at the game, it's like, well, pick New York's.

00:20:35.880 --> 00:20:38.820
If you don't think about strategy, you can just make sure you hit the ball.

00:20:38.960 --> 00:20:43.040
Which is, in NES baseball games, sometimes a hard enough challenge on its own.

00:20:43.300 --> 00:20:47.760
So what did you guys think of the, I know that we don't really talk about this with baseball games.

00:20:47.760 --> 00:20:51.920
Because it's a bit more on rails than a typical, you know, NES game.

00:20:52.280 --> 00:20:55.360
Uh, but what did you think of the AI in this game?

00:20:55.360 --> 00:21:05.120
Um, the only thing I really noticed was kind of what I already mentioned, was that, like, they were not accounting for the same difficulties I have, like, not being able to see your fielders.

00:21:05.280 --> 00:21:08.700
But I don't really think I picked up on anything, like, specific.

00:21:08.940 --> 00:21:11.400
I think they were kind of stupid, though, on other things.

00:21:12.080 --> 00:21:14.600
I think you could trick them on base running a lot.

00:21:14.600 --> 00:21:21.620
Not, or, like, not even, like, with squeeze plays or anything, but just, um, what was a good example?

00:21:21.800 --> 00:21:29.360
Like, if you, if you know that they are, if the guy in right field is throwing all the way to third base, right?

00:21:29.460 --> 00:21:30.580
Like, that's a long stretch.

00:21:30.580 --> 00:21:38.560
It's like, I can send back, like, the appropriate runner that I need to, or I can move forward as I please.

00:21:38.560 --> 00:21:46.260
There's a lot of, like, just a lot of, like, it's out of their hands now, and it's obvious that they're either going to make the throw or they're not.

00:21:46.400 --> 00:21:51.860
But even weird things, like, plays to first base, I feel like they weren't, like, 100% on.

00:21:51.860 --> 00:21:53.880
Yeah, there was a lot more throwing to home before first.

00:21:54.100 --> 00:22:01.260
Yeah. I just never really see, but I think the, what I brought this up for is, is a bit more comical.

00:22:02.700 --> 00:22:09.760
Um, I was, I, I was just screwing around, and I noticed that, oh, hit by pitch.

00:22:09.920 --> 00:22:14.360
Like, they can, there's a lot of times where it just looked like I was about to get hit by a pitch, but I didn't.

00:22:14.440 --> 00:22:19.640
So I just kept kind of, like, climbing closer and closer to the plate, and then I eventually got hit by a pitch.

00:22:19.740 --> 00:22:21.000
And I'm like, oh, that's fun, that's cute.

00:22:21.140 --> 00:22:23.040
Like, he does a weird little cartoon cry.

00:22:23.040 --> 00:22:33.840
Um, and then they move him over to base, and then I just, like, stayed there, like, hovering over the plate, and I got hit by pitch four times in a row.

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:37.320
I scored on four consecutive hit by pitches.

00:22:37.640 --> 00:22:39.540
And just, like, are they just on rails?

00:22:39.720 --> 00:22:45.080
Like, where the, like, is it just, like, a prescribed, uh, pitch, uh.

00:22:45.520 --> 00:22:47.540
Yeah, it's, like, determined before the game even starts.

00:22:47.540 --> 00:22:54.100
Exactly. Uh, it was just so strange, because I've never even seen that in any other, I've never seen that in any other baseball video game.

00:22:54.620 --> 00:22:57.860
And it's, uh, it's illegal in Major League Baseball play.

00:22:58.000 --> 00:22:59.980
You have to move out of the way.

00:23:00.300 --> 00:23:03.020
If the, like, you can't stand there and take the hit.

00:23:03.200 --> 00:23:06.640
It actually just counts as a strike at that point.

00:23:07.040 --> 00:23:11.580
See, I mean, that makes sense with people playing, but I guess they didn't account for that.

00:23:11.680 --> 00:23:17.280
Well, you know, Sean, this reminds me of a story about my own experience in Little League Baseball.

00:23:18.140 --> 00:23:21.020
And, um, basically the story goes like this.

00:23:21.240 --> 00:23:24.800
Uh, kids are not, uh, athletes yet.

00:23:25.060 --> 00:23:26.660
Okay? They are athletes in training.

00:23:27.220 --> 00:23:37.240
And, uh, there comes a time where first we start with the tee ball, and then we move into, you know, coach gets to pitch to the kids, and he, like, lobs it on their hand.

00:23:37.480 --> 00:23:43.400
And then eventually, you know, one cool guy says, like, I want to be the pitcher, and he pitches.

00:23:44.020 --> 00:23:47.340
And you maybe have, like, one or two of those guys on your entire team.

00:23:47.540 --> 00:23:54.480
But kids get gassed a lot easier, and there's a lot of pressure to be pitching when you're, like, nine years old or whatever.

00:23:54.980 --> 00:24:03.260
And so, uh, this kid on the team was the pitcher, completely spent, nobody else to go throw anymore.

00:24:03.260 --> 00:24:05.200
And the other team just realized it.

00:24:05.480 --> 00:24:06.760
And you know what they did?

00:24:07.060 --> 00:24:09.000
They stopped swinging for the rest of the game.

00:24:09.960 --> 00:24:13.580
And they walked in until the runs came, until we basically had a four-fit.

00:24:13.580 --> 00:24:14.720
That is tough for that pitcher.

00:24:14.980 --> 00:24:16.140
That's really funny.

00:24:16.140 --> 00:24:25.020
It's, it's, it's terrible, it's a terrible story, but, like, that's what's happening here, is, like, those, those pitchers that you were up against, Sean, were just spent, and all they knew how to do was hit ya.

00:24:25.440 --> 00:24:29.460
Yeah. But there's, like, a mercy rule.

00:24:29.580 --> 00:24:30.860
Like, this game is a mercy rule.

00:24:31.460 --> 00:24:34.220
But that's, like, a real thing in Little League Baseball, right?

00:24:34.320 --> 00:24:36.800
Yeah, I think it's, um, I think it's a lot of runs, though.

00:24:37.280 --> 00:24:39.480
I think it's, like, five or seven runs.

00:24:39.980 --> 00:24:41.400
I think in this it's ten.

00:24:41.940 --> 00:24:43.420
Okay, maybe it's ten in general.

00:24:43.780 --> 00:24:46.700
Um, but, you know, that, that happened to me, too.

00:24:46.920 --> 00:24:48.200
That's a, that's a whole nother story.

00:24:50.860 --> 00:24:53.780
I've been on every aisle of the Little League, uh, baseball.

00:24:54.240 --> 00:24:55.100
You were a pitcher?

00:24:55.760 --> 00:24:58.720
Uh, no. Uh, I didn't say position, I said aisle.

00:24:59.300 --> 00:25:03.620
Um, because I was also, you know, I actually played right field.

00:25:03.780 --> 00:25:07.660
That was, that was where my example was earlier, was if you're in right field and you throw a third base.

00:25:07.780 --> 00:25:09.600
It was, like, I, I wasn't making it.

00:25:10.020 --> 00:25:11.140
Wasn't completing that throw.

00:25:11.320 --> 00:25:15.280
That's too far. Now, I'll throw it to the second baseman for the relay.

00:25:15.660 --> 00:25:17.840
Yeah. Yeah, you gotta get cut off in there.

00:25:17.840 --> 00:25:18.940
Yeah, right, exactly.

00:25:19.780 --> 00:25:21.100
Uh, where was I going with this?

00:25:21.300 --> 00:25:27.040
Uh, you know, since we're talking about, like, little unique features, uh, you, there's also the shift is in this game.

00:25:27.140 --> 00:25:30.080
And also now, like, a legal feature in baseball.

00:25:30.320 --> 00:25:32.120
But you can do, uh.

00:25:32.420 --> 00:25:40.480
That's crazy. Yeah, you can do an infield or outfield shift, uh, which is just, like, I don't know, pretty heads up for Little League.

00:25:40.880 --> 00:25:47.180
Yeah. You know, like, sometimes I feel like this game is more, uh, put together than, like, bases loaded was.

00:25:47.180 --> 00:25:49.380
And, and meanwhile, it's just a little bit of baseball.

00:25:49.380 --> 00:25:52.600
Yeah, it definitely wasn't, you couldn't shift in that game, right?

00:25:52.860 --> 00:25:54.220
I, I don't recall.

00:25:54.620 --> 00:25:56.700
Uh, definitely not in, like, an RBI baseball.

00:25:57.180 --> 00:25:58.100
Yeah, absolutely.

00:25:58.400 --> 00:26:07.440
It was, it was really weird when, like, I, I pressed a button and started cycling through menus on the, uh, uh, on the pitching screen.

00:26:07.440 --> 00:26:11.360
